  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nRiver flooding continues along the Big Blue and Turkey Creek,\nwith the crest now having passed downstream of Crete but still\nupstream of Beatrice. The Missouri River continues to fall as\nthe crest has moved south of Rulo.\n\nThe large scale weather pattern features rather zonal flow\noverhead with slight ridging in the southwestern CONUS and\nnosing up the west coast. A compact short wave trough evident in\nwater vapor imagery was quickly moving across South Dakota \ntoday. This trough, with its associated low pressure and \ntrailing cool front, have produced an area of precipitation \nacross eastern SD into MN. This precipitation is moving off to \nthe east, and while the front will move into northeast Nebraska \nthis afternoon, precipitation is unlikely this far south as the \nsub-cloud layer is very dry. That said, there could be a \nsprinkle or two, but minimal impact overall. Otherwise, winds \nwill decrease as the afternoon progresses, and will turn out of \nthe northwest this evening as the cool front moves through. \n\nThe next few days will be mostly sunny with highs in the 60s to\nlower 70s through Monday, and a bit warmer after that as the\nwestern ridging builds a bit more into our region. We'll keep an\neye on the Thursday/Friday time frame for some potential showers\nin the warm advection regime of a weak storm system, but as of\nnow the forecast through the next 7+ days is a dry one, with a\ngeneral model consensus in a longer wave trough potentially\nbringing more unsettled weather to the region as we move into\nthe Day 9-11 period.",
